WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:05.500
Over the past two years,

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:08.220
how many times have you heard
that the times have changed

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:09.660
or about the new normal?

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.180
There’s no shortage of opinions

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.260
about whether remote work
is for the better

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.860
or spells destruction for our businesses.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:19.620
Reed Hastings, CEO of Netflix, said,
"I don't see any positives,"

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:23.540
whereas Suresh Kumar, CTO of Walmart,
said, "We haven't just coped.

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:25.220
We've actually thrived."

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.420
But what does the research say?

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:32.220
How can we know, not just think,
that hybrid working actually works?

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:34.700
There are so many questions.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:38.540
What's the right balance of work from home
versus work from the office?

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:39.700
And who gets to decide?

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:41.900
Is it managers, is it the employees?

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.260
Is it both?

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:46.860
How long can a given employee actually
remain productive working from home?

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:48.300
Is there a limit?

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.460
And who gets to work in each way?

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:52.980
What should it be based on
in terms of criteria?

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:56.500
Should it be based on seniority,
task, personal situation?

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:58.340
The list goes on and on.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.620
So many questions and unfortunately,

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:01.980
no simple answers.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.620
The reason these questions
are so hard to answer

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:08.740
is because we're thinking about hybrid
work design as a single problem.

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:14.420
One problem to solve, when in fact,
it's actually three different ones.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:18.420
The first problem is
are we able to effectively deliver

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.260
on our stakeholder commitments?

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:22.820
This is otherwise known
as the effectiveness debate.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:27.860
The second is will we be able to attract
and retain the talent that we need?

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.380
This is the staffing discussion.

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:35.780
And the third is can we maintain
or even cultivate, nurture, our culture?

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:38.020
This is the talk about social fabric.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:42.100
Let's take a minute to highlight
the challenges inherent in each one.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:46.060
I've been working with a range
of organizations over the past two years,

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:49.260
helping them determine what
their future of work should look like.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.940
Now, I'm not here
to make a broad statement

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:54.900
that remote work is
or is not universally good.

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.740
I think we're all
smart enough here to know

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:00.100
that nothing works all of the time.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:05.780
I am, however, here to warn you
that we need to be wary of our data.

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.020
Let me give you a couple of examples.

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:11.700
Many people point to their organization's
effectiveness during COVID

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.860
as proof that they're actually
good at remote work.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:20.260
Now it is proof that remote can work,
but not that it necessarily will.

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:24.220
COVID was a massive social experiment
with unique conditions.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:27.060
Most organizations dealt with it
by cutting the fat

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:30.900
and becoming laser-focused
on short-term efficiency.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:35.180
In effect, we were productive
because we were in survival mode.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:38.460
But the question we have to ask
is whether that's sustainable.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:40.380
Data shows around the world

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:42.860
that people's working hours
have increased,

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.060
and many say that they
actually find it harder

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:47.340
to delineate work-life boundaries.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.460
Data also shows that the experience
wasn't the same for everyone.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:54.780
With “The Economist” data showing
that parents of school-aged children

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:57.340
experienced much greater stress
than many others.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:01.300
And data from Microsoft shows
that it's actually even changing the way

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:02.620
in which we work,

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:06.020
with people working more hours
but less collaboratively.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:09.660
The question of whether
we can effectively work remotely

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:11.820
needs a contextual answer.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.820
Contextual based on the people
who are doing the work

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:17.100
and the tasks that they're
trying to accomplish.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:19.660
Remember, one size fits none,

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:21.940
and we need to think
about the sustainability

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:23.460
of our effectiveness.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:25.660
Now, when it comes
to the staffing discussion,

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:28.860
ask anyone who has recently
been involved with hiring.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:32.140
The most common question that recruiters
are being asked these days:

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:34.740
"What is your flexible-work policy?"

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:37.780
In effect, we’re facing
the same escalation of perks

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:40.580
popularized by tech companies
during the boom.

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:43.740
Do you have a barista pulling
the perfect flat white in the lobby?

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:46.740
Do you have nap pods?
A ball pit? A slide?

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:48.820
What about on-site daycare, right?

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:52.220
So then instead of work from home,
we actually bring home into work.

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:57.500
Your current or potential future employees
are now weighing your hybrid work policy

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:01.740
as a key criteria in their decision
of where they want to work.

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:04.420
And that is the crux
of this staffing challenge.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:06.340
What you need to recognize

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:10.500
is that the comparison isn't actually
between work from home and office work.

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:14.140
Instead, it's really between
the perception of work from home

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:16.340
versus the perception
of work from the office.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:18.980
And you need to reclaim that narrative.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.260
Let me give you a couple of examples.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.540
The first is what I call
the recovered commute.

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:24.980
Many people have told me,

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:28.420
"I've saved so much time
now that I don't have to commute."

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:30.620
My challenge to them?

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:33.900
What did you actually do
during your commute times?

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:36.580
Maybe you read, maybe you caught up
on calls or emails,

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:39.660
maybe you just use it
as time to decompress.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:43.420
Personally, I used to get an hour
to shake off a particularly rough,

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:46.860
annoying, frustrating day
before I got home.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:50.740
Now it takes me exactly six seconds
to be immersed in my family upstairs.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:55.900
Another example, the after-meeting
postmortem debrief over a coffee, right?

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:58.100
We use that to do some
relationship repair,

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:00.220
maybe to do some collective sense-making.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:02.940
The most important thing to recognize here

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:05.100
is that what matters most
is the experience,

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:07.460
not just what the policy is.

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:11.500
We need to reclaim the narrative to help
ensure that everybody recognizes

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:14.780
what a given approach
either buys or loses for them.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:18.060
And this brings us to the social fabric.

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:21.300
Think about what happened
when you joined your organization.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:22.900
You probably went to orientation.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:24.260
Maybe you looked around,

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:26.180
you talked to some people, you observed

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:28.620
and you learned
what it's like to work here.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:29.780
Why does that matter?

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:32.300
Well, because our research
shows reductions in things

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:35.740
like psychological safety and trust,
changes to power dynamics,

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:38.140
increased feelings
of isolation and loneliness

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:40.620
when we are working
remote from one another.

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:43.740
Importantly, all of these
shape our cultures

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:46.380
and make this conversation
even more difficult.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:48.100
And on top of that,

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:52.620
social systems are dynamic,
emerging, evolving human systems.

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:56.220
Now, I'm going to be honest,
I don't have the answer to this.

00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:58.340
And honestly, anybody
who tells you they do,

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:00.100
they're trying to sell you something.

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:02.820
We do know about different
approaches to building

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:05.220
and establishing culture
in these contexts.

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:08.740
What we have to recognize is they operate
in ways that may not be the same

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:11.420
as the way we built culture
when we were face to face.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:13.140
What we have to remember here

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:15.900
is that organizational culture
is a long game.

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:19.940
What we do right now affects
the social fabric of our organizations

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:22.020
and will have repercussions down the line.

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:24.380
So when it comes to the social fabric,

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:27.060
we need to think not only about today,

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:30.540
but about tomorrow, next month,
maybe even next year.

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:34.940
I hope you recognize that these are three
distinct conversations

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:37.020
that are also not fully independent.

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:39.820
We also have to recognize
what makes this challenging

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:42.540
is that they are almost
ideologically different positions

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:45.460
about what creates value
in your organization.

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:47.860
Is it about the output
of what you produce?

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:49.940
Is it about the people
in that organization

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:51.940
or something in the ether, the culture?

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:55.900
The first most important step
is getting these issues on the table,

00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:57.260
having an open conversation.

00:06:57.260 --> 00:06:58.980
And this is far from easy.

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:04.300
But if you find yourself in disagreement
with somebody over these issues,

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:07.820
whether it's a boss, a subordinate
or your leadership,

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:10.100
I challenge you, ask yourself,

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:13.500
do you really disagree
on how to create effectiveness,

00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:16.540
deal with staffing or the culture
of the organization?

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:20.820
Or is it maybe just that you have
slightly different prioritization

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:24.420
of those three different parts
of the whole hybridity conversation?

